text(await tools.mcp__codex_app__send_message_to_thread({threadId:"01a106a1-1a12-79a0-a0ce-3d23f204db4f",hostId:"local",prompt:"Create a reviewable Slack gateway PRD for Clanker in the yyot repository, preserving unrelated work. User explicitly requests a PRD to review before implementation. Do not implement gateway code, deploy, provision paid resources, push/create a PR, change Slack settings, post messages, or change tldraw. A previous conversational PR request was superseded by clarification of the actual problem and this PRD-first request. Use a suitable isolated worktree for file changes if appropriate; read applicable instructions and skill. File should be a clear named Markdown document (e.g. SLACK_GATEWAY_PRD.md), draft status, and open it in Codex when ready. You own prior gateway research and should use its inspected source evidence.

Full intent: Clanker v1 hosted Slack browser assistant reusing Hermes, but experience is the contract and Hermes should be adapted to it. User wants v1 quickly; do not make throughput ceiling/load benchmarking or multi-gateway replica research prerequisite. Architecture is ONE shared Slack gateway, not multiple gateways or a shared Hermes agent across users. That gateway receives Slack events, validates tenant/workspace/sender/audience, durably accepts and routes tasks. Ten events from ten different users must become ten owner/context-scoped tasks routed to their respective isolated Hermes task runtimes, subject to bounded worker capacity and fair queuing. Each assigned worker runs Hermes + Browser Use driver + local Chromium. Profile history, browser state and files persist outside worker lifetime, loaded for authorized context when task begins and checkpointed durably. One active run per profile; shared channel/thread context remains audience-scoped. Scheduler/pool manager chooses workers; define a clean boundary rather than assuming all scheduling is inside adapter. Replies, progress and approvals return through shared gateway and must associate with original task and permitted Slack destination. Private observations never automatically flow to shared channels; exact owner/content/audience approval is separate. Gateway must not fall back to privileged profiles, accept model-supplied identity as authority, or expose broad Slack/cloud credentials to agent workers.

User thinks stock Slack adapter cannot implement dynamic per-task pool dispatch unchanged. Accurate finding: existing listener/event/reply functionality may be reusable, while dispatch lifecycle must be adapted; do not claim all connector code must be rewritten or stock proxy solves design. Prior inspected Hermes pinned 0.21.5 adapter is Socket Mode, normal Python runtime. Runs API is candidate worker integration supporting admission/status/events/approval/steering/stop/idempotency; API scope isn't automatically Slack owner. Stock dedup is in-memory and stock approval admission lacks complete ownership guarantees. Clearly distinguish source-verified findings from untested deployment behavior.

Provider preference Cloudflare; Codex connected but account paid capabilities unverified. Persistent gateway allowed. Previously proposed mapping: one persistent Cloudflare Container for gateway using Socket Mode; Workers for trusted backend/access UI; Queues dispatch; Durable Objects scheduler/pool coordination and durable structured state; Containers isolated task workers; R2 scoped profile/browser/files. This is provisional, not fully approved implementation. The real requirement is dynamic authorized worker dispatch, not switching Socket Mode to HTTP. Discuss transport/hosting as implementation choices; recommend minimal approach with rationale, make any consequential open decisions explicit, and keep unrelated service selection out of scope. Socket Mode receives inbound Slack events; replies use Slack APIs regardless of inbound transport. User willing to modify Hermes or custom connector only as needed for required experience.

Read current V1_SCOPE.md, VERIFICATION_STRATEGY.md and pinned source as needed. PRD should define concrete user journeys, gateway responsibilities/nonresponsibilities, trusted task/event/result envelope semantics, identity/routing/private/shared context, durable acceptance + dedup, progress/completion delivery, pending human input/access resume, exact owner approvals/cancellation, recovery/uncertain external outcomes, boundary with scheduler/vault/worker, minimal v1 acceptance scenarios (including ten distinct users conceptually; label synthetic vs real), reuse/modification boundary, initial service/transport recommendation and unresolved decisions. Do not turn PRD into a full SaaS platform or require operator/provider privacy controls deferred by v1. Preserve manual-vs-auto TOTP as outside gateway scope. Keep it focused and reviewable. Report document absolute path, concise decisions needing review, and any genuine blocker. No implementation before user's review."}));
