text(await tools.mcp__codex_app__send_message_to_thread({threadId:"01a106a1-1a12-79a0-a0ce-3d23f204db4f",hostId:"local",prompt:"Create a polished, shareable architecture diagram for Clanker using OFFICIAL Cloudflare service icons. This is an aesthetics/documentation task explicitly requested by the user, not authorization to implement, provision, deploy, publish externally, or send anything to Sahan/Nishank. User understood the previous flow and wants much lower visual cognitive load: official service icons, very short responsibility captions, attractive layout suitable to share with Sahan and Nishank. Preserve architecture and indicate it is proposed v1 architecture, not a verified deployed system. PRD remains draft awaiting review. Do not change the PRD or implementation.

The existing tldraw board is 'clanker — architecture draft', boardId yxsZ5oF5q6AEIF-1lop6j, URL https://www.tldraw.com/f/yxsZ5oF5q6AEIF-1lop6j. Read through tldraw plugin APIs. Prefer add a new clearly titled presentation page on this same board and preserve the original detailed draft page, rather than destructively replacing it. Use official icons from Cloudflare-published assets, documentation or official repositories; browse primary sources to locate exact icons for Workers, Containers, Durable Objects, Queues, R2. Do not invent icons or call third-party approximations official. If a product lacks a published usable icon, mark that limitation and use an accurately sourced generic Cloudflare compute icon with explicit label only if needed; aim to locate the real assets first. Use exact vector assets where possible, not generated imitations. Deliver a local high-resolution PNG preview plus editable SVG if practical, and the editable tldraw presentation page. If tldraw image handling is impractical, create a standalone SVG/PNG/HTML artifact and show it; do not block the requested diagram on tldraw limitations. Use appropriate design tooling/skills as helpful. Files belong in the yyot project, preserve dirty work, follow AGENTS and CLI help/man rule. No auto posting or external sharing.

Architecture to depict faithfully:
ONE shared Slack gateway: adapted Hermes Python Slack handling in a persistent Cloudflare Container, Socket Mode inbound; replies use Slack Web API. This is separate from per-task agent execution. Gateway submits authenticated task over HTTP to Clanker Backend in Cloudflare Worker. Backend includes HTTP endpoints and queue consumer. Backend persists accepted task in Scheduler Durable Object and publishes recoverable notification/task reference to Cloudflare Queues. Queues invokes backend queue consumer; backend passes task to scheduler via internal binding. Scheduler is OUR code running in Durable Object, owns durable tasks, assignments, profile leases; one active run per profile. Pool manager and per-worker container controllers are OUR code using Durable Objects and Container API, maintain bounded clean worker capacity, start/select/release workers. Assigned isolated Cloudflare Container runs Hermes + Browser Use + local Chromium, one authorized task/context; not shared Hermes across tenants/users. Private R2 stores authorized profile history/browser state/files/artifacts beyond worker lifetime, restored into local working filesystem with consistent checkpoints back (R2 isn't live SQLite disk). Agent container progress/completion goes over authenticated HTTP to backend; backend validates assignment, updates scheduler state and delivers permitted replies through shared gateway. Durable completion/checkpoint releases assignment so next task can run. Ten users' tasks dispatch to distinct authorized task runtimes as capacity permits; no multiple Slack gateways in this design.

For presentation, simplify arrows and group components into clear areas: Slack entry; trusted coordination (gateway/backend/queue/scheduler/pool); isolated task execution; durable state. Use visual arrows for main task path, return progress/results, and restore/save state without a hairball. Main diagram captions around 3-8 words; a compact legend can explain container vs serverless vs storage if helpful. Explicitly label OUR scheduler/pool code versus Cloudflare infrastructure, but don't clutter with API jargon. It is acceptable to group scheduler + pool manager visually under Durable Objects while keeping responsibilities distinguishable. Illustrate a small worker pool if helpful, making each task/context isolated; ONE shared Slack gateway. Include vault/access flow only if needed for complete original board: selected Workers vault website with Sign in with Slack, separate trusted vault Container and encrypted R2 storage with keys outside agent execution, but latest discussed diagram focuses task routing/execution; do not silently overexpand. Prefer task routing/execution diagram first, title scope clearly.

Return source URLs for official assets, exact absolute artifact paths, tldraw link/page name, brief note on any icon substitution or unverified architectural assumption. Open preview in current owning chat if possible. Parent will promote PNG and links into voice timeline; ensure files actually exist and image is visually inspected for readability/correctness. Do not send a long technical narration; deliver the visual."}));
